AwZmIwNTc4ZWUwOTMwYzkzMDJhNDI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Vadymk95/template-1/commit/6e47f3d94567379e0ed71ad08e3bf8e1ee511d6c","sha":"6e47f3d94567379e0ed71ad08e3bf8e1ee511d6c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vadymk95/template-1/commits/6e47f3d94567379e0ed71ad08e3bf8e1ee511d6c"}],"sha":"6cd8b7f9b1d256f24a00fb0578ee0930c9302a42","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vadymk95/template-1/commits/6cd8b7f9b1d256f24a00fb0578ee0930c9302a42"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/70842045?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Vadymk95","id":70842045,"login":"Vadymk95","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcwODQyMDQ1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vadymk95","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vadymk95/template-1/commits/6e47f3d94567379e0ed71ad08e3bf8e1ee511d6c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-13T20:06:07Z","email":"70842045+Vadymk95@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Vadym Kononenko"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-13T20:06:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ci): a fork that renames its default branch no longer loses CI and protection (#70)\n\nThe ruleset targets ~DEFAULT_BRANCH, which follows a rename. The workflow triggers\ndid not: they named `master` literally. So a fork whose owner renamed the default\nbranch to `main` got a ruleset demanding a check that no workflow would ever run,\nand every pull request in that repository blocked forever with no error anywhere.\nThe trigger filters now name both branches, which is what this template''s React\nNative sibling already did.\n\n`.github/ruleset.json` is new: settings do not travel with a fork, so the only way\nto hand one over is as a file the forker can post back. It carries the check names\nthese workflows actually produce, so renaming a CI job means editing this file in\nthe same commit. README now points at it with the exact command instead of naming\none check in prose, which is also how the same sentence stops being wrong in the\nsibling whose check is not called `validate`.\n\nRelease: the header comment claimed no CI runs on a release pull request. Measured\ntoday, the runs are created and sit in `action_required`, reporting nothing, which\nis why a release PR shows zero checks and \"Merging is blocked\". The comment now\nsays that, names the one-click fix and the PAT, and says not to reach for an admin\nbypass, which is not selective and would drop the status checks, the pull-request\nrule and the force-push rule together.\n\n`.npmrc` takes the audit floor of `high` that the RN sibling documented: the\nblocking gate fails on high and critical, so a bare `npm audit` set to `moderate`\nreports a severity the gate will not block.","tree":{"sha":"1a15e1fa5e8fc02da874ce25758ea84f70bedceb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vadymk95/template-1/git/trees/1a15e1fa5e8fc02da874ce25758ea84f70bedceb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vadymk95/template-1/git/commits/6e47f3d94567379e0ed71ad08e3bf8e1ee511d6c","verification":{"payload":"tree 1a15e1fa5e8fc02da874ce25758ea84f70bedceb\nparent f5a873169835f08a6a9606cf7723562145b599ff\nauthor Vadym Kononenko <70842045+Vadymk95@users.noreply.github.com> 1789329967 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789329967 +0300\n\nfix(ci): a fork that renames its default branch no longer loses CI and protection (#70)\n\nThe ruleset targets ~DEFAULT_BRANCH, which follows a rename. The workflow triggers\ndid not: they named `master` literally. So a fork whose owner renamed the default\nbranch to `main` got a ruleset demanding a check that no workflow would ever run,\nand every pull request in that repository blocked forever with no error anywhere.\nThe trigger filters now name both branches, which is what this template''s React\nNative sibling already did.\n\n`.github/ruleset.json` is new: settings do not travel with a fork, so the only way\nto hand one over is as a file the forker can post back. It carries the check names\nthese workflows actually produce, so renaming a CI job means editing this file in\nthe same commit. README now points at it with the exact command instead of naming\none check in prose, which is also how the same sentence stops being wrong in the\nsibling whose check is not called `validate`.\n\nRelease: the header comment claimed no CI runs on a release pull request. Measured\ntoday, the runs are created and sit in `action_required`, reporting nothing, which\nis why a release PR shows zero checks and \"Merging is blocked\". The comment now\nsays that, names the one-click fix and the PAT, and says not to reach for an admin\nbypass, which is not selective and would drop the status checks, the pull-request\nrule and the force-push rule together.\n\n`.npmrc` takes the audit floor of `high` that the RN sibling documented: the\nblocking gate fails on high and critical, so a bare `npm audit` set to `moderate`\nreports a severity the gate will not block.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqpwIvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGIgQADfLJGnAnB6589evzVFFPcYx\nC8EpE66SZ24pHCn775P+73S7lcWSexzikAGzQfUDuPg85g6D7z1tMHclvM6tzkMQ\n7pBsUWIl8CEPZkW3y76VG5gWC4HHN/Tn2YGoASxKBqGDbmRMDcZ3iY67L9AIbWSq\nHfBHZLVSpXc8MG1AegIMO2rClKoOKvwPVnIoLJrkk42qoW0ulHeJoJSyfKeKcY0k\n1g2wIc0DUcUP3pKBISYcX1C